WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.360
- Hey, it's me Gina.

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.580
I'm here with Queer Eye's
Tan who's flown over

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:06.143
from the States to talk
everything British.

00:00:07.209 --> 00:00:09.709
(theme music)

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.940
What was the biggest culture
shock or the hardest thing

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.620
to get adjusted to when you moved?

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.090
- Easy, driving on the
wrong side of the road.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:23.300
And I'm using my words correctly,
wrong side of the road.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.350
We drive on the correct side
of the road, the left side.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:28.419
- Exactly.

00:00:28.419 --> 00:00:30.630
(laughs)

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.240
Which British stereotypes

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.300
do you have to get Americans to overcome?

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.893
- That we are always polite,
that we are passive-aggressive,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.790
and that we don't swear.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.330
Every time I just think, how
are we not used to this yet,

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:45.163
my husband?

00:00:45.163 --> 00:00:46.680
- My husband. (laughs)

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.250
- Well, it's been 11 years.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.450
- And what stereotypes have
you learned to discount

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.330
about Americans?

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.130
- That they're phony.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:58.090
They seem so cheesy that I
assumed that it was an act,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:58.923
but they're not.

00:00:58.923 --> 00:01:01.580
Most Americans I meet,
they're really friendly

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.430
and engaging and truly do
wanna know about your life.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.340
It's lovely.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.470
- But loud.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.820
- Very loud, but I think that
there are just so many of them

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.670
and it's such a large country
that they've just learned

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.243
that that's how they are heard.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.187
- I don't think it's actually
a bad way to be at all.

00:01:16.187 --> 00:01:17.160
(laughs)

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.040
- I mean I've become louder
ever since I got out there.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.640
I just need my opinion to be heard,

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.220
so I'm gonna speak loudly now.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.006
- I think I'd fit right
in with it. (laughs)

00:01:25.006 --> 00:01:27.328
- Yeah, but no, but I
love that you're the one

00:01:27.328 --> 00:01:28.928
who is saying that they're loud.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.503
- I'm quite (mumbles).

00:01:31.503 --> 00:01:33.280
- Ha, ha, I've noticed.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.760
- Which is better, American
cookies or British biscuits?

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.020
- Oh, British biscuits.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:39.853
- [Gina] Yeah, I agree.

00:01:39.853 --> 00:01:40.686
I was so worried you were
gonna say the cookies.

00:01:40.686 --> 00:01:42.860
- I love a hobnob so much.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.100
- Ooh, good choice.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.700
- What's your biscuit of choice?

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.180
- You know what, custard cream?

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.013
Is that all right?

00:01:48.013 --> 00:01:48.846
- I love a custard cream.

00:01:48.846 --> 00:01:49.980
- [Gina] I love custard creams.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.030
- And also a Bourbon.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:53.410
- I'm out, I'm so sorry.
(gasps)

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:54.260
I don't like Bourbons.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:55.930
What, the chocolate tastes weird.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.500
- It does taste weird.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.210
It absolutely tastes weird, it
doesn't taste like chocolate,

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.160
but it's so good.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.568
Ooh, and have you also tried a fig roll?

00:02:02.568 --> 00:02:04.510
- Ooh, yeah, I have but you're giving me.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:05.770
That's like Grandpop biscuit, no?

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:07.528
- I know, I'm older than you.

00:02:07.528 --> 00:02:08.600
(laughs)

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.433
- I'm just saying. (laughs)

00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:10.363
- Shady bitch.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.930
- Tell me your opinions on American gravy.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.020
- I, whenever I'm in
England, when I go back home

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:21.400
I often will bring an empty suitcase

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.380
and I will fill it with Bisto and stuffing

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.080
and all those things that I
still don't get back home.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.170
- British fashion or American fashion.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.150
- Ooh, British.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.570
And the reason why I say British

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.777
is because our High Street is wicked.

00:02:35.777 --> 00:02:36.800
Wicked, wicked.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:37.870
- Do you have a favorite High Street shop?

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.540
Which one would you go for?

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.240
- Oh God, that's a tough question.

00:02:42.232 --> 00:02:43.630
Bershka.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:44.859
- [Gina] Ooh!

00:02:44.859 --> 00:02:45.692
- I love Bershka.

00:02:45.692 --> 00:02:46.560
- That was a plot twist,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:47.420
I didn't think you were gonna say Bershka.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.253
- I love Bershka.

00:02:48.253 --> 00:02:49.700
- Wasn't about that, okay, yeah.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:50.533
- Do you like Bershka.

00:02:50.533 --> 00:02:51.366
- I do like Bershka.

00:02:51.366 --> 00:02:53.980
But I find that a few people say that.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.220
- When I wear something from Bershka,

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.230
I don't see everybody else in it.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:00.070
Like I literally, legitimately
twice have worn Bershka

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:02.910
on a red carpet and people think
it's something super fancy.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.740
But no, it costs like 25 quid.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:05.640
- Oh my goodness.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.930
- Yeah, I mean don't them at the time.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.160
But later on if somebody asks.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.580
- Where would you tell
an American friend to go

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.560
if they had a week in the UK?

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.043
- As much as I love London,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.130
probably not London because
I think you can get this vibe

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:22.750
in many other capital cities.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.970
I would suggest going up north.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:29.010
I'd suggest hitting up
Manchester for the music scene.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:33.300
York for just the
experience, the Cotswolds,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.220
the lake district.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.410
There's so much more to England

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:39.630
than what we see in global press or media.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.033
It's not just London.

